WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.450
This is an age where disruptions
are taking place at such a rapid pace.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.950
Where the company can go from leading
to bleeding at the blink of an eye.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.470
&gt;&gt; It's really about
designing to be agile.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.765
Designing to anticipate the unknown.

00:00:14.765 --> 00:00:19.320
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.070
Almost all solutions to problems out
there have a technology base to it.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.150
And it is the CIO who

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.460
has to be leading the charge in terms
of coming up with innovative solutions.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.640
The CIO program is geared towards CIOs
leveraging technology to drive change

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.410
across organizations.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.480
What we want to do is to have everyone
see the CIO not as a cost center but

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.990
as a solution center.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:47.570
&gt;&gt; From strategic vision development,
innovation, scaling excellence,

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:49.760
this program will make me a better leader.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.000
&gt;&gt; This program exposes CIOs to

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.070
the cutting edge tools that
are available out there.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:59.401
For example, design thinking or behavior
design, to cutting-edge frameworks,

00:00:59.401 --> 00:01:01.880
concepts, best practices out there.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.090
&gt;&gt; When we brainstorm,
we put up an idea and

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.300
who ever can shoot down the idea
first is the genius in the room.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.960
And through design thinking,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.330
really picked up some great ways
to do brainstorming better.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.110
How do we get more rapid
in terms of prototyping and

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.190
testing things rather than spending
six months kind of building something,

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.860
only to get the feedback
after we launch it?

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.890
&gt;&gt; Something as simple as understanding
that we make decisions generally 95%

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.450
of the time based on emotion and instinct.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.430
It's very fundamental and

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.346
will absolutely carry value going forward
in probably all aspects of my life.

00:01:33.346 --> 00:01:37.980
It's the all-star content
from the GSB no doubt, and

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.520
then on top of it, it's in Silicon Valley.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.830
In one week you really do get to
kind of capture the essence of what

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:43.620
it's like here.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.890
&gt;&gt; One of the great aspects of this
program is that we have time for

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.900
discussion so that others who have similar
problems can learn from one another.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.655
&gt;&gt; It is a completely immersive week.

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:56.640
[LAUGH] And it's joyfully intense.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.130
&gt;&gt; Changes that are going to take
place in the future in organizations

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.726
in the marketplace are going
to be led by CIO's.

00:02:03.726 --> 00:02:06.296
&gt;&gt; I think all of these
skills will allow more and

00:02:06.296 --> 00:02:10.559
more CIOs to sit at the table with other
C-level executives as being really

00:02:10.559 --> 00:02:13.217
critical in driving
innovation in a company.

